    source_trajectory_step_info {
      trajectory_id: "e551cd62-4338-4894-9dae-599aeb4b5a3e"
      step_index: 2
      cascade_id: "e551cd62-4338-4894-9dae-599aeb4b5a3e"
    }
    model_api_content_id: "a3e8824708934cb78a00cc82c08ed7ca"
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "Build a responsive website called \"Abandoned but Not Forgotten\": an interactive 3D museum of NASA hardware left on the Moon, on Mars and in deep space. Audience: school-age space enthusiasts. All text in English.\n\n## CORE FEATURE\n\n- A rotatable, zoomable 3D globe using the globe.gl library. Load it from https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/globe.gl and pin an exact version.\n- Planet switcher: Moon / Mars.\n- Use placeholder texture URLs in one config object so I can replace them with NASA imagery later.\n- No atmosphere on the Moon.\n- Each exhibit is a marker at its landing-site latitude/longitude.\n- Hovering over a marker shows its name and years.\n- Clicking a marker opens a side panel, full-screen on mobile, containing:\n  - name\n  - years\n  - status stamp: STILL WORKING / SILENT / ENDED ON PURPOSE\n  - photo\n  - \"The story\" \342\200\224 3\342\200\2234 sentences\n  - \"What we learned\"\n  - \"Hardware in simple words\"\n  - a small mono data table\n  - source link\n- The globe smoothly flies to the clicked marker.\n\n### Deep Space mode\n\n- Add a third mode called \"Deep Space\".\n- In this mode, do not show the globe.\n- Show Voyager 1, Pioneer and Cassini as an editorial list/diagram.\n- Include distance from Earth, status and the same detail panel used for planetary exhibits.\n\n## CHALLENGE REQUIREMENTS\n\nThe site must visibly include the following:\n\n### Planned vs Reality\n\nCreate a section called \"Planned vs Reality\" with comparisons of planned and actual lifetime:\n\n- Ingenuity: planned 5 flights \342\206\222 actual 72 flights\n- Opportunity: planned 90 days \342\206\222 actual about 14 years\n\n### Guess the machine\n\nCreate a short quiz called \"Guess the machine\" with 5 questions.\n\n- Each question describes a machine using information from its story.\n- Provide 4 answer options.\n- The player selects an answer.\n- Show whether the answer is correct.\n- Keep track of the score.\n- At the end, show the final score.\n- Let the player restart the quiz.\n\n### Closing line\n\nEnd the website with exactly this line:\n\n\"These machines were left behind. We have not forgotten them.\"\n\n## CONTENT AND DATA\n\n- All exhibit content must be stored in one data array at the top of the code.\n- Each exhibit object must contain:\n  - id\n  - zone\n  - name\n  - years\n  - status\n  - lat\n  - lng\n  - story\n  - learned\n  - hardware\n  - planned\n  - actual\n  - source\n  - image\n- Use placeholder exhibit data for now.\n- Use gray placeholder images for now.\n- Keep all content in English.\n\n## TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Mobile-first and responsive.\n- Keyboard accessible.\n- Good color contrast and readable text.\n- Fast loading.\n- No backend.\n- No login.\n- Use semantic HTML where appropriate.\n- Keep the implementation self-contained and easy to modify.\n- Clearly separate configuration, exhibit data, application logic and UI components.\n- Make it easy to replace placeholder images and planet textures later.\n- Do not hard-code exhibit information throughout the application; use the central data array.\n- The website should work as a standalone front-end project.\n- Include all necessary HTML, CSS and JavaScript needed to run the site.\n- Use the exact pinned version of globe.gl rather than an unversioned CDN URL.\n- Do not add unnecessary libraries or dependencies."
    items {
      text: "Build a responsive website called \"Abandoned but Not Forgotten\": an interactive 3D museum of NASA hardware left on the Moon, on Mars and in deep space. Audience: school-age space enthusiasts. All text in English.\n\n## CORE FEATURE\n\n- A rotatable, zoomable 3D globe using the globe.gl library. Load it from https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/globe.gl and pin an exact version.\n- Planet switcher: Moon / Mars.\n- Use placeholder texture URLs in one config object so I can replace them with NASA imagery later.\n- No atmosphere on the Moon.\n- Each exhibit is a marker at its landing-site latitude/longitude.\n- Hovering over a marker shows its name and years.\n- Clicking a marker opens a side panel, full-screen on mobile, containing:\n  - name\n  - years\n  - status stamp: STILL WORKING / SILENT / ENDED ON PURPOSE\n  - photo\n  - \"The story\" \342\200\224 3\342\200\2234 sentences\n  - \"What we learned\"\n  - \"Hardware in simple words\"\n  - a small mono data table\n  - source link\n- The globe smoothly flies to the clicked marker.\n\n### Deep Space mode\n\n- Add a third mode called \"Deep Space\".\n- In this mode, do not show the globe.\n- Show Voyager 1, Pioneer and Cassini as an editorial list/diagram.\n- Include distance from Earth, status and the same detail panel used for planetary exhibits.\n\n## CHALLENGE REQUIREMENTS\n\nThe site must visibly include the following:\n\n### Planned vs Reality\n\nCreate a section called \"Planned vs Reality\" with comparisons of planned and actual lifetime:\n\n- Ingenuity: planned 5 flights \342\206\222 actual 72 flights\n- Opportunity: planned 90 days \342\206\222 actual about 14 years\n\n### Guess the machine\n\nCreate a short quiz called \"Guess the machine\" with 5 questions.\n\n- Each question describes a machine using information from its story.\n- Provide 4 answer options.\n- The player selects an answer.\n- Show whether the answer is correct.\n- Keep track of the score.\n- At the end, show the final score.\n- Let the player restart the quiz.\n\n### Closing line\n\nEnd the website with exactly this line:\n\n\"These machines were left behind. We have not forgotten them.\"\n\n## CONTENT AND DATA\n\n- All exhibit content must be stored in one data array at the top of the code.\n- Each exhibit object must contain:\n  - id\n  - zone\n  - name\n  - years\n  - status\n  - lat\n  - lng\n  - story\n  - learned\n  - hardware\n  - planned\n  - actual\n  - source\n  - image\n- Use placeholder exhibit data for now.\n- Use gray placeholder images for now.\n- Keep all content in English.\n\n## TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Mobile-first and responsive.\n- Keyboard accessible.\n- Good color contrast and readable text.\n- Fast loading.\n- No backend.\n- No login.\n- Use semantic HTML where appropriate.\n- Keep the implementation self-contained and easy to modify.\n- Clearly separate configuration, exhibit data, application logic and UI components.\n- Make it easy to replace placeholder images and planet textures later.\n- Do not hard-code exhibit information throughout the application; use the central data array.\n- The website should work as a standalone front-end project.\n- Include all necessary HTML, CSS and JavaScript needed to run the site.\n- Use the exact pinned version of globe.gl rather than an unversioned CDN URL.\n- Do not add unnecessary libraries or dependencies."
    }
  }
}
