  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Project Requirements**\n\nOkay, I\'m meticulously analyzing every detail of this prompt. I\'m focusing on the \"Abandoned but Not Forgotten\" website concept \342\200\223 an interactive 3D museum of NASA\'s abandoned space hardware. I am drilling down on the key components and audience. I am striving for 100% precision.\n\n\n**Refining Metadata & Globe**\n\nI\'ve got the project\'s target audience and language confirmed as school-age space enthusiasts and English, respectively. I am now updating `metadata.json` and `index.html` to reflect the project\'s title, \"Abandoned but Not Forgotten,\" and its description, per the instructions in `<environment_constraints>`. I\'m also ensuring the correct version of globe.gl, specifically 2.35.1, is pinned for the 3D globe integration.\n\n\n**Addressing CDN Dependencies**\n\nI\'m now focusing on robustly handling the `globe.gl` library dependency. I\'ve ensured the CDN script tag is in `index.html` to load it externally. I\'m also architecting the Globe component to dynamically inject the script if needed and use `window.Globe` when available. Furthermore, I\'m integrating a backup Three.js interactive fallback, and making sure images load and don\'t stall, or it can swap to a procedural canvas texture. I\'m building out a `CONFIG` object for textures.\n\n\n**Implementing Texture Fallbacks**\n\nI\'m now implementing robust fallback mechanisms for the globe textures. I\'m focusing on ensuring the visual fidelity of the 3D globe, even if external URLs fail. My thought is that `CONFIG` will hold URLs for textures, including placeholders. I am integrating procedural canvas fallback textures for the Moon and Mars for a crisp, high-detail display that avoids a black sphere in the event of load failures.\n\n\n**Optimizing Texture Loading**\n\nI\'m now refining the texture loading process for the 3D globe. Specifically, I\'m integrating checks before using texture URLs from `CONFIG`. I\'m going to test if an image loads before passing the texture URL to `globeImageUrl()`, or using it as the default. If the placeholder host is unreachable, I\'ll provide a high-resolution, procedural, equirectangular canvas data URL fallback for a hermetic environment. This keeps the `CONFIG.textures` URLs central and directly connected for immediate globe updates. Even if they\'re default placeholder URLs, the procedural fallback will render clean planetary grids or load the user\'s updated URL.\n\n\n**Verifying Texture Configuration**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the texture configuration and data array structure. I\'m verifying that `CONFIG.textures.moon` and `CONFIG.textures.mars` are directly used in `.globeImageUrl()`. I\'m creating placeholder URLs that can be easily replaced. I\'m integrating gray placeholder images per the prompt. I\'m also preparing for the inspection of the single data array containing all exhibit content, ensuring each exhibit object adheres to the specified structure including the exhibit attributes.\n\n\n**Structuring Data Organization**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the data organization strategy, driven by the prompt\'s emphasis on separation and a single source of truth. I am ensuring that the `CONFIG` and `EXHIBITS` are declared at the very top of `src/data/museumData.ts` and re-exported from `src/data/index.ts`. I am also ensuring import or a commented section is placed at the top of `src/App.tsx`. Placeholder exhibit data and images will be used initially, adhering to the gray placeholder image convention and easy replacement strategy. No hardcoding is occurring; everything references the central array.\n\n\n**Confirming Data Placement**\n\nI\'m now implementing the final stage for data location. I\'ll declare `CONFIG` and `EXHIBITS` at the top of `src/App.tsx`, under clear banners, to satisfy modular UI requirements. This strategy will allow immediate access to inspectors, with no imports to trace. This approach also allows re-exporting. I\'ll pass them to the modular UI components as props. I\'m verifying that the data structure fulfills both requirements of organization and access.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Deep Space Navigation**\n\nI\'m now implementing the deep space navigation functionality. I will create an editorial list for Voyager 1, Pioneer, and Cassini, omitting the globe view for this mode. I\'ll include the distance from Earth, status information, and a detail panel mirroring the planetary exhibits. On the Moon, I will explicitly disable the atmosphere using `.showAtmosphere(false)`. For Mars, I\'ll enable a subtle atmosphere with `.showAtmosphere(true).atmosphereColor(\'#d97742\').atmosphereAltitude(0.12)`.\n\n\n**Integrating Marker Functionality**\n\nI\'m now integrating robust marker interactivity, ensuring both keyboard accessibility and visual clarity. I\'m focusing on making the markers interactive buttons using both `htmlElementsData` and `pointsData` in `globe.gl`. I\'m incorporating hover tooltips for each marker, displaying the name and years. On click (or keyboard activation), the camera will smoothly fly to the exhibit\'s location, opening the side panel simultaneously. I\'m also adding a keyboard-accessible exhibit index list for easy navigation.\n\n\n**Verifying Status Parameters**\n\nI\'m now implementing the exhibit status strings, ensuring that each exhibit uses the required `status` values: `\'STILL WORKING\'`, `\'SILENT\'`, or `\'ENDED ON PURPOSE\'`. I\'ll review and validate the existing data for exact string matches and ensure semantic styling and clear text rendering in the UI. I am now working through the exhibit data.\n\n\n**Validating Exhibit Data**\n\nI\'m now implementing exhibit data validation to ensure data integrity and adherence to the specified structure. I am confirming that each exhibit object rigorously uses the required fields: `name`, `years`, `status`, `lat`, `lng`, `story`, `learned`, `hardware`, `planned`, `actual`, `source`, and `image`. I\'m going through the data checking it field by field, making sure that it all fits.\n\n\n**Validating Placeholder Integrity**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on placeholder integrity. I\'m ensuring all exhibits use gray placeholder images, either inline SVGs or configurable generators, marked for easy replacement. I\'m confirming that the `image` property in the exhibit data is set to a placeholder. I\'m also reviewing the `CONFIG` for planet texture URLs to verify placeholders and easy replacement.\n\n\n**Refining Configuration & Globals**\n\nI\'m currently creating the `CONFIG` object, organizing planet textures to use NASA imagery later, as indicated. I\'ve also confirmed that `globe.gl`\'s `THREE.TextureLoader` supports both standard image URLs *and* data URLs. I will be incorporating this information in my work. The data URLs, in the end, will be gray/tinted placeholder textures, either inline SVGs or data URLs. I plan to use the `createPlaceholderPlanetTexture` helper for this.\n\n\n**Prioritizing URL Flexibility**\n\nI\'m now implementing the ability for users to replace placeholder URLs directly in the `CONFIG` object, providing a clear and editable structure for NASA imagery integration. I am also planning robust image load checks in the `GlobeViewport` component with fallbacks to avoid any network disruptions or slow loads and keep the globe running smoothly.\n\n\n**Implementing Deep Space Mode**\n\nI am now implementing the \"Deep Space\" mode. I\'m focusing on excluding the globe and showing an editorial list for Voyager 1, Pioneer 10, and Cassini, as requested. The list will incorporate distance from Earth, status, and the same details panel as planetary exhibits. I am updating `EXHIBITS` with the requisite deep space objects, ensuring they include their respective distance from Earth and status details.\n\n\n**Finalizing Deep Space View**\n\nI\'m now completing the deep space view implementation. I\'ve integrated an editorial list with distances and statuses for Voyager 1, Pioneer 10, and Cassini, omitting the 3D globe. I am verifying that the deep space objects are set and that their statuses are represented as appropriate. I am also implementing the interactive **Deep Space Distance Diagram (Heliocentric / AU Scale Diagram)** showing Earth, Saturn (Cassini at ~9.5 AU), the Kuiper Belt / Heliosphere boundary, Pioneer 10 (~134 AU), and Voyager 1 (~164 AU in interstellar space). I am also confirming the correct positioning of these items relative to each other.\n\n\n**Deriving Data Metrics**\n\nI\'m now deriving the \"Planned vs Reality\" section directly from the `EXHIBITS` array. I\'ll dynamically generate comparisons for planned versus actual lifetimes, including specific examples like Ingenuity and Opportunity, and will ensure it renders as requested. The UI panel will render as requested. I\'ll make sure to display each exhibit\'s information correctly.\n\n\n**Constructing Quiz Mechanics**\n\nI\'m now building the \"Guess the Machine\" quiz. My plan is to dynamically create the quiz questions from the existing `EXHIBITS` data, ensuring it remains connected to the exhibit details. I will also make sure the quiz is engaging for school-aged space enthusiasts. I plan to build out keyboard accessible answers. I will incorporate a mechanism to track the score, and show the final result, and let the user restart the game.\n\n\n"
