  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Design Principles**\n\nI\'m currently reviewing the typography and aesthetic rules within the `frontend-design` skill, specifically referencing materials from `8_science_space_biotech.md` and `9_museum_editorial_institutional.md`. I am focusing in on the top bar contract, specifically, a single row design, split into three zones.\n\n\n**Refining the Top Bar**\n\nI\'ve finalized the top bar\'s structure: a single row split into three zones. Zone one will house the brand title, zone two navigation links, and zone three the primary action button. I\'m choosing fonts: Fraunces, Plus Jakarta Sans, and JetBrains Mono. Color discipline is prioritized: deep space obsidian, flat slate, and calibrated telemetry amber/laser cyan will dominate the look. I am also working on \"Anti-Slop & Zero-Pill Discipline,\" so no distracting elements will be added.\n\n\n**Designing Status Stamps**\n\nI\'m now focused on status stamps, considering a crisp museum archival style. I plan to use rectangular technical stamps with distinct icons or glyphs and clear text indicators, like `\342\227\217 STILL WORKING`. Metadata will be displayed as clean unboxed text with separators. I am ensuring that the architecture cleanly satisfies all project requirements. I\'ve updated the metadata.json and index.html files accordingly.\n\n\n**Defining Exhibit Types**\n\nI am creating type definitions for the exhibits. I\'ve defined `ZoneType` as \'Moon\', \'Mars\', or \'Deep Space\'. I\'ve also defined `ExhibitStatus` as \'STILL WORKING\', \'SILENT\', or \'ENDED ON PURPOSE\'. The `Exhibit` interface now includes all required fields like `id`, `zone`, `name`, `years`, `status`, `lat`, `lng`, etc. I\'ve also included optional technical fields for the data table and Deep Space mode.\n\n\n**Establishing Data Structures**\n\nI\'m now focusing on setting up the initial data structures. I\'ll define `CONFIG` and `EXHIBITS` at the top of `src/App.tsx`, and re-export them in `src/data/exhibits.ts` for cleaner component imports. I\'ve incorporated placeholder texture URLs in a configuration object, ready for NASA imagery, and a central data array for all exhibit content. I will also use gray placeholder images for now. I\'m ensuring a clear separation between configuration, data, logic, and UI components.\n\n\n**Populating Exhibit Data**\n\nI\'m now populating the exhibit data. I have added details for \'Moon\' and \'Mars\' exhibits, including titles, dates, statuses, and coordinates for each. I\'ve also incorporated latitude and longitude data, now ready for spatial integration.\n\n\n**Adding Exhibit Data**\n\nI\'ve just added several more exhibits to my data set. These include details on missions like Opportunity, Curiosity, Perseverance, Sojourner, InSight, and Voyager 1. Each now has a defined status, latitude, longitude, and operational dates.\n\n\n**Integrating Globe.gl**\n\nI\'m now integrating `globe.gl`. I\'ve added data on Pioneer 10 and Cassini Orbiter exhibits, including distance from Earth. I am checking the globe integration by loading it into the `index.html` file, within the `GlobeViewport` component, specifically on mount. I\'ll focus on smooth rendering and responsive scaling next.\n\n\n**Generating Planet Textures**\n\nI\'m now generating planet textures. I\'m focusing on creating placeholder textures for `globe.gl` that are robust. To ensure they work in sandboxed environments, I\'m generating an SVG data URL for each planet. This will bypass external image host dependencies, while providing a default fallback. I\'ll test these local SVG placeholders before considering other alternatives, like `placehold.co`, which might be blocked.\n\n\n**Creating Placeholder Textures**\n\nI\'m now generating planet textures. I\'m focusing on creating robust placeholder textures for `globe.gl`. I\'ll generate an SVG data URL for each planet, ensuring that they work in sandboxed environments and bypass external image host dependencies, while providing a default fallback. I\'ll test these local SVG placeholders before considering alternatives. I also want to define an `export const CONFIG` with those images.\n\n\n**Refining Exhibit Images**\n\nI\'m now refining exhibit image handling and considering placeholders. I have confirmed that using `createPlanetPlaceholderTextureUrl(...)` creates valid `data:image/svg+xml;utf8,...` URLs that `globe.gl` loads immediately without network issues, displaying grid lines and contours.  I can quickly swap this for a NASA image. Similarly, exhibits\' images now use `createGrayExhibitPlaceholderUrl(...)` for crisp gray placeholders that are easily replaced. I\'m ensuring this is as efficient and reliable as possible.\n\n\n**Implementing Globe Markers**\n\nI am now focusing on implementing the markers on `globe.gl` using `htmlElementsData`. I\'ll render an interactive HTML `<button>` for each exhibit at its specific `lat`/`lng`. These buttons will be keyboard-focusable. Inside each marker, there will be a high-contrast target pin and status indicator, to satisfy the requirement of hovering to show its name and years. I\'ll handle the atmosphere for Mars and the Moon, turning it off for the latter. Finally, I will ensure exhibits are correctly loaded, and that clicking the marker will correctly fly to the location on the globe.\n\n\n**Developing Interactive Markers**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in developing interactive markers for `globe.gl` using `htmlElementsData`. I\'ve rendered interactive HTML buttons for each exhibit at their specified `lat`/`lng` coordinates. These buttons are keyboard-focusable and each includes a target pin and status indicator for high contrast. Hovering highlights the marker and displays a tooltip card with its **name** and **years** (and coordinates), while clicking smoothly flies the globe camera to the exhibit\'s location and opens the side panel, perfectly satisfying all project requirements. I have also integrated `.ringsData()` or `.pointsData()` for the same functionality on the globe.\n\n\n**Addressing Side Panel**\n\nI\'m now addressing the side panel requirements, triggered by a marker click. It will open full-screen on mobile and desktop, showing all exhibit data and technical fields, as well as enabling smooth camera transitions to match each exhibit location. I will integrate this with the existing implementation of globe marker clicks.\n\n\n**Constructing Side Panel Data**\n\nI\'m now constructing the side panel content, which is triggered by marker clicks. It will include all required exhibit data: name, years, status stamp, photo, the story, what we learned, hardware details, a mono data table and source link. The data table uses the following keys: Zone, Active Years, Status, Coordinates / Position, Planned Lifetime, Actual Lifetime. On mobile this side panel will be full screen.\n\n\n\n"
    tool_calls {
